<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Personent hodie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">usually dated from a manuscript of <a href="/years/1360">1360</a>; first printed <a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Personent hodie&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> first published in 1582 in Piae Cantiones, a Finnish song book of 74 medieval songs with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts, collected by Jacobus Finno (Jaakko Suomalainen), a Lutheran cleric, and published by T. P. Rutha.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Piae Cantiones, in which the carol appeared, had its origins in the libraries of cathedral song schools, whose repertory had strong links with medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where clerical students from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had studied for generations. A highly similar melody is found in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1360 from Moosburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and the song is usually dated from that manuscript.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text is probably a musical parody of an earlier 12th-century song, &quot;Intonent hodie voces ecclesie&quot;, written in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>. Hugh Keyte and Andrew Parrott note that two verses have an unusual double repeat, &quot;Submersum, -sum, -sum puerum&quot; and &quot;Reddens vir-, vir-, vir-ginibus&quot;. In the earlier song these illustrated the three boys and three girls whom Nicholas saved from drowning and from prostitution. The text was probably rewritten for the Feast of the Holy Innocents on 28 December, when choristers and their boy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> traditionally displaced the senior clergy from the choir stalls, and the carol is still often associated with that day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Songs from the Piae Cantiones continued to be performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> until the 19th century. A rare copy owned by Peter of Nyland was given to the British Minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, who gave it to John Mason Neale in 1852. Neale and Thomas Helmore published songs from it in two collections, in 1853 and 1854.</p><p>The usual English translation, &quot;On this day earth shall ring&quot;, is by &quot;James M. Joseph&quot;, a pseudonym of the composer Jane M. Joseph (1894–1929). Elizabeth Poston made another in 1965, and Neale's &quot;Let the Song be Begun&quot; uses the melody without the text. The carol became better known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after Gustav Holst arranged it in 1916 for unison voices and orchestra; its organ reduction is often a processional hymn, and the arrangement has been sung in the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols at King's College Chapel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p><p>Keyte and Parrott harmonised it for the New Oxford Book of Carols, and John Rutter made two arrangements. Maddy Prior and The Carnival Band recorded it in 1987, Anonymous 4 sang it with its original &quot;Intonent hodie&quot; text, and Wayne Shorter recorded his arrangement of the melody as 12th Century Carol in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Personent hodie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Personent hodie</b>&quot; is a Christmas carol first published in 1582 in Piae Cantiones, a Finnish song book of 74 medieval songs with Latin texts, collected by Jacobus Finno (Jaakko Suomalainen), a Lutheran cleric, and published by T. P. Rutha.</blockquote><a href="/songs/personent-hodie">Personent hodie</a></li></ul></section>
